{"amendments":[{"body":"Fix the exact-zero DIC discontinuity in the carbonate solver. `crates/tank_core/src/systems/chemistry.rs:145-147` returns `NEUTRAL_FALLBACK` whenever DIC reaches zero, but the same solver returns `pH = 8.5` for arbitrarily small positive DIC under the same alkalinity. Because live update paths clamp DIC to exactly zero before resolving (`crates/tank_core/src/systems/chemistry.rs:304-313`, `crates/tank_core/src/systems/plant_growth.rs:147-149`, `crates/tank_core/src/systems/algae_growth.rs:112-115`, `crates/tank_core/src/systems/algae_growth.rs:193-196`), a strongly photosynthetic tank can jump from the alkaline ceiling to neutral in one tick. The zero-DIC branch should use a limit-consistent fallback instead of snapping to `pH 7.0`.","rationale":"This is a user-visible chemistry error, not just an edge-case: the surrounding systems can legitimately drive DIC to exactly zero, and I reproduced the current solver returning 8.5 at 0.001 mg C/L but 7.0 at 0 mg C/L for the same 1.5 meq/L buffer."},{"body":"Unify source-water carbonate validation and runtime resolution onto one authoritative contract. `crates/tank_data/src/presets.rs:44-46` and `crates/tank_data/src/presets.rs:150-169` reject zero-alkalinity / nonzero-DIC source waters via a separate `predicted_carbonate_ph` implementation, while runtime source-water validation in `crates/tank_core/src/types/source_water.rs:31-65` accepts the same chemistry and materialization resolves it through `crates/tank_core/src/types/water.rs:87-110` and `crates/tank_core/src/systems/chemistry.rs:74-79` / `crates/tank_core/src/systems/chemistry.rs:152-156`. Right now the same source-water payload can be rejected when loaded from TOML but accepted when it arrives via a save or runtime catalog, which means preset loading and simulation no longer share one carbonate contract. Move both paths to the same helper/validation rule.","rationale":"This is a correctness and robustness problem at the API boundary: the behavior of source-water chemistry now depends on how it entered the system, so preset files, saves, and runtime catalogs can disagree about whether a profile is valid and what pH it should produce."}],"summary":"Reviewed the changed chemistry, save/migration, source-water, scenario, and shrimp-routing code end-to-end, and ran targeted `tank_core` chemistry/shrimp tests plus a local solver repro. I found 2 substantive issues: an exact-zero DIC pH discontinuity that can fire in live growth paths, and divergent source-water carbonate validation between preset loading and runtime materialization."}